  "id": "DrugBank_DB00338 - MONDO_1409",
  "verbalization": "Omeprazole is implicated in the pathogenesis of esophagitis through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Omeprazole influences gastrin secretion, which in turn affects the development of esophagitis. This is supported by the indication of Omeprazole to gastrin secretion abnormality, followed by the indication of gastrin secretion abnormality to drugs like Roxatidine Acetate and Ranitidine, both of which are linked to esophagitis. These pathways highlight a potential mechanism by which Omeprazole may contribute to esophagitis, likely through its effects on gastric acid secretion and related gastrointestinal functions. The integration of these pathways underscores a role for Omeprazole in modulating gastric and esophageal physiology, potentially leading to the development of esophagitis. The evidence, while not directly linking Omeprazole to esophagitis, provides a coherent network of interactions that support a broader mechanism of action.",
